{{define "title"}}Terms of use - MPP Viewer{{end}}

{{define "main"}}
<main class="page page--text legal centered">
  <div class="page__head">
    <h1>Terms of use</h1>
  </div>

  <p class="legal__note">
    <strong>Draft.</strong> The facts below describe exactly how the service behaves today.
    The operator's legal name, governing law, liability wording and the paid-plan terms still have
    to be filled in, and the document reviewed, before this page can be published.
  </p>

  <h2>What the service does</h2>
  <p>
    MPP Viewer reads Microsoft Project files - <code>.mpp</code> and Project <code>.xml</code> -
    and shows the schedule as a Gantt chart in the browser. It can also export the schedule to an
    Excel spreadsheet. It is a reader: it does not change your file and cannot write one back.
  </p>

  <h2>Using it without an account</h2>
  <p>
    Opening and converting a plan needs no account. Nothing is stored, so nothing can be returned
    to you later. An account is needed only to keep plans and to share them by link.
  </p>

  <h2>What you may upload</h2>
  <p>
    Only files you have the right to use. Do not upload anything you are not permitted to process
    outside your organisation. You keep every right in your own plans; the service claims none of
    them and does not use their contents for anything but showing and converting them for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Sharing</h2>
  <p>
    A shared plan is readable by anyone holding the link - with the password, if you set one.
    Choosing to share is your decision and your responsibility. Sharing can be revoked at any time
    and the link stops working immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <p>
    Uploads are capped by size, and uploads and sign-in attempts are limited in frequency, so that
    one visitor cannot exhaust the service for everyone. Free accounts may share a limited number
    of plans at a time; the paid plan lifts that limit and raises the upload size.
  </p>

  <h2>Paid plan</h2>
  <p><strong>[price, billing period, refund and cancellation terms]</strong></p>

  <h2>Availability and liability</h2>
  <p>
    The service is provided as it is, without a guarantee of uninterrupted availability. Keep your
    own copy of anything that matters: a plan stored here is a convenience, not a backup.
  </p>
  <p><strong>[liability limits and governing law]</strong></p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p><strong>[legal name, address, contact address]</strong></p>
  <p class="legal__meta">Last updated: <strong>[date]</strong></p>
</main>

